# Google Cloud Ops Agent Monitoring – Alert Configuration

## 1. Purpose

This document defines the monitoring approach for Google Cloud Ops Agent running on Compute Engine MFT servers.

The monitoring must detect two separate failure scenarios:

1. **Ops Agent stops or stops reporting telemetry**
2. **Ops Agent remains active, but logs are no longer reaching Cloud Logging**

These scenarios require separate alerting signals because a healthy `systemd` service state does not guarantee successful log ingestion.

---

# 2. Environment

Validated environment:

```text
Operating System: Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8.10
Ops Agent Package: google-cloud-ops-agent
Ops Agent Version: 2.70.0
Architecture: x86_64
```

The installed version can be checked with:

```bash
yum info google-cloud-ops-agent
```

The Ops Agent parent service can be checked with:

```bash
sudo systemctl status google-cloud-ops-agent --no-pager
```

The parent service might appear as:

```text
Active: active (exited)
```

This is expected. The actual telemetry processing is handled by Ops Agent component services.

For the installed version, the relevant services include:

```bash
sudo systemctl status google-cloud-ops-agent-opentelemetry-collector --no-pager
sudo systemctl status google-cloud-ops-agent-fluent-bit --no-pager
```

Both should normally show:

```text
Active: active (running)
```

Note: Starting with Ops Agent 2.66.0, the default logging pipeline can be handled by the Metrics Agent/OpenTelemetry-based service rather than exclusively by Fluent Bit. Therefore, monitoring only the Fluent Bit process is not sufficient to validate log delivery. ([Google Cloud Documentation][1])

---

# 3. Monitoring Design

Two alerts should be implemented:

| Alert                          | Signal                                                                | Detects                                               |
| ------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| Ops Agent Not Reporting        | `agent.googleapis.com/agent/uptime`                                   | Agent/component stopped or no longer reporting        |
| Ops Agent Log Shipping Failure | `agent.googleapis.com/agent/log_entry_count` using `ops-agent-health` | Agent running but logging path is not delivering logs |

The two alerts complement each other.

```text
                    Compute Engine VM
                           |
                     Google Ops Agent
                      /            \
                     /              \
          Agent telemetry        Log telemetry
                 |                    |
          agent/uptime       LogPingOpsAgent
                 |                    |
                 v                    v
             Alert 1              Alert 2
```

---

# 4. Alert 1 – Ops Agent Not Reporting

## Objective

Generate an alert when Cloud Monitoring stops receiving the Ops Agent uptime metric from an MFT VM.

The metric is:

```text
agent.googleapis.com/agent/uptime
```

This metric is generated by the Ops Agent and is sampled approximately every 60 seconds. ([Google Cloud Documentation][2])

The alert should use **metric absence**, because when the Ops Agent fails, the metric typically disappears rather than reporting a value of zero.

---

## 4.1 Verify the metric first

Before creating the alert:

Go to:

```text
Google Cloud Console
→ Monitoring
→ Metrics Explorer
```

Switch to:

```text
Code
→ PromQL
```

Run:

```promql
rate(
  {
    "__name__"="agent.googleapis.com/agent/uptime",
    monitored_resource="gce_instance"
  }[1m]
)
```

Google recommends this query when troubleshooting whether Ops Agent components are sending telemetry. ([Google Cloud Documentation][3])

Confirm that the target MFT VM appears in the results.

---

# 4.2 Create the alerting policy

Go to:

```text
Google Cloud Console
→ Monitoring
→ Alerting
→ Create policy
```

Select:

```text
Select a metric
```

Search for:

```text
agent.googleapis.com/agent/uptime
```

Resource type:

```text
VM Instance
```

Monitored resource:

```text
gce_instance
```

---

# 4.3 Filter the target MFT VMs

Apply a filter that uniquely identifies the required MFT VM or MFT VM group.

For initial implementation, use:

```text
instance_id = <MFT_VM_INSTANCE_ID>
```

Example:

```text
instance_id = 1234567890123456789
```

If multiple MFT servers are monitored, each VM must remain an independently evaluated time series.

Avoid averaging all VM metrics into one time series because one failed agent could be hidden by healthy agents on other VMs.

---

# 4.4 Configure the alert condition

Configure:

```text
Condition type:
Metric absence
```

Use:

```text
Trigger:
Any time series violates

Metric absence duration:
5 minutes
```

Recommended configuration:

```text
Metric:
agent.googleapis.com/agent/uptime

Resource:
gce_instance

Condition:
Metric absence

Absence duration:
5 minutes

Trigger:
Any time series violates
```

The resulting behavior is:

```text
Ops Agent reporting
       |
       v
agent/uptime received approximately every minute
       |
       X
Agent stops reporting
       |
       v
No uptime metric for 5 minutes
       |
       v
Cloud Monitoring incident created
```

Metric-absence conditions are specifically designed to detect a time series that stops producing expected data. ([Google Cloud Documentation][3])

---

# 4.5 Alert name

Recommended:

```text
PROD | MFT | Ops Agent Not Reporting
```

Avoid naming the alert simply:

```text
Ops Agent Down
```

because metric absence proves that Cloud Monitoring is no longer receiving Ops Agent telemetry. It does not necessarily prove that the Linux process itself has crashed.

Other causes could include:

```text
Ops Agent failure
Monitoring pipeline issue
IAM/permission issue
Network connectivity issue
Monitoring API issue
Telemetry exporter failure
```

