# 5. Alert 2 – Ops Agent Log Shipping Failure

## Objective

Detect the situation where the Ops Agent is running but logs are no longer successfully reaching Cloud Logging.

This is important because the following can occur:

```text
systemctl status = active
```

while:

```text
Logs are not reaching Cloud Logging
```

Process monitoring alone does not detect this failure.

---

# 5.1 LogPingOpsAgent health check

Starting with Ops Agent version **2.46.0**, Google introduced an informational health check:

```text
LogPingOpsAgent
```

It writes an informational message approximately every **10 minutes** into:

```text
ops-agent-health
```

The purpose is specifically to verify that the Ops Agent is successfully writing logs. ([Google Cloud Documentation][4])

Our validated Ops Agent version is:

```text
2.70.0
```

Therefore this functionality is available.

---

# 5.2 Verify LogPingOpsAgent first

Go to:

```text
Google Cloud Console
→ Logging
→ Logs Explorer
```

Run:

```text
resource.type="gce_instance"
log_id("ops-agent-health")
jsonPayload.code="LogPingOpsAgent"
```

For a specific MFT VM:

```text
resource.type="gce_instance"
resource.labels.instance_id="<INSTANCE_ID>"
log_id("ops-agent-health")
jsonPayload.code="LogPingOpsAgent"
```

Expected behavior:

```text
Approximately every 10 minutes:

LogPingOpsAgent
```

Google recommends using these messages to validate that the Ops Agent is successfully writing logs. ([Google Cloud Documentation][4])

Do not create the alert until the heartbeat has been observed for the target VM.

---

# 5.3 Create the log-shipping alert

Go to:

```text
Google Cloud Console
→ Monitoring
→ Alerting
→ Create policy
```

Select:

```text
Select a metric
```

Search for:

```text
log entries
```

Select:

```text
Resource type:
VM Instance

Metric category:
Logs-based metrics

Metric:
Log entries
```

This corresponds to the Ops Agent metric:

```text
agent.googleapis.com/agent/log_entry_count
```

Google explicitly documents `log_entry_count` as the metric to use for monitoring the `LogPingOpsAgent` heartbeat. ([Google Cloud Documentation][4])

---

# 5.4 Configure filters

Filter by the target VM:

```text
instance_id = <MFT_VM_INSTANCE_ID>
```

Also filter:

```text
log = ops-agent-health
```

Conceptually:

```text
instance_id = <MFT_VM_INSTANCE_ID>

AND

log = ops-agent-health
```

This ensures the condition is based on the Ops Agent's own health messages rather than application logs.

---

# 5.5 Transform the data

Configure:

```text
Rolling window:
10 minutes

Rolling window function:
delta
```

`log_entry_count` is cumulative, so `delta` calculates how many new health log entries were written during the evaluation window.

Expected normal behavior:

```text
10-minute window
       ↓
At least one LogPingOpsAgent entry
       ↓
delta >= 1
       ↓
Healthy
```

---

# 5.6 Configure alert trigger

Configure:

```text
Condition type:
Threshold

Threshold position:
Below threshold

Threshold:
1

Alert trigger:
Any time series violates

Retest window:
No retest
```

Configuration summary:

```text
Metric:
Log entries / agent log_entry_count

Resource:
VM Instance

Filter:
instance_id = <MFT_VM_INSTANCE_ID>
log = ops-agent-health

Rolling window:
10 minutes

Rolling function:
delta

Condition:
Below threshold

Threshold:
1

Trigger:
Any time series violates
```

Google documents this approach for alerting when a VM stops updating the Ops Agent `log_entry_count` heartbeat metric. ([Google Cloud Documentation][4])

---

# 5.7 Alert name

Recommended:

```text
PROD | MFT | Ops Agent Log Shipping Failure
```

This is more accurate than:

```text
Ops Agent Down
```

because this alert detects loss of the log-delivery heartbeat while the Ops Agent process itself might still be running.

---

# 5.8 Alert documentation / runbook

Recommended:

```text
The Ops Agent logging heartbeat has stopped updating for the affected
MFT VM.

The Ops Agent service might still appear active, but the logging pipeline
might not be successfully sending logs to Cloud Logging.

Investigation:

1. Check Ops Agent services:

   sudo systemctl status google-cloud-ops-agent-* --no-pager

2. Review recent Ops Agent errors:

   sudo journalctl \
     -u 'google-cloud-ops-agent*' \
     --since "30 minutes ago" \
     --no-pager

3. Check specifically for logging/API errors:

   sudo journalctl \
     -u 'google-cloud-ops-agent*' \
     --since "30 minutes ago" \
     --no-pager \
     | grep -Ei 'LogApi|LogPipe|error|failed|permission|403|retry'

4. Verify the LogPingOpsAgent event in Cloud Logging:

   resource.type="gce_instance"
   resource.labels.instance_id="<INSTANCE_ID>"
   log_id("ops-agent-health")
   jsonPayload.code="LogPingOpsAgent"

5. Validate VM network connectivity and required Google APIs.

6. Validate the VM service account has the required Logging permissions.

7. If required, restart the Ops Agent:

   sudo systemctl restart google-cloud-ops-agent

8. Verify LogPingOpsAgent entries resume.
```

Ops Agent runtime health checks can report conditions such as connectivity, API and permission failures, which are useful when troubleshooting this state. ([Google Cloud Documentation][4])

